+
+## Capture continuity — proposed isolated patch
+
+Before publishing the first frame of a reopened camera or changed native geometry, capture reports an unthrottled `source_epoch` event. Disconnect reports are also unthrottled. Bootstrap sends source status synchronously to the preview before forwarding it to the launcher. The preview immediately invalidates that source’s queued observations, keeps other cameras intact, and checks the epoch both before JPEG encoding and after temporary-file writing. Only the final guarded rename holds the state lock; capture never waits for JPEG encoding or file serialization. Late worker output from a previous epoch cannot become a fresh-looking picture. Inference and snapshot-processing status records carry the analyzed capture epoch too. Once the launcher knows a camera epoch, results with a different or missing epoch cannot establish readiness or refresh processing activity. The status limiter retains only the last time/epoch per kind, source and rule, so an old result cannot suppress the first result from a new connection.
+
+The session heartbeat communicates this bounded per-source state using the existing transport. Browser visibility can lag behind capture invalidation until the next heartbeat/poll, and an unusually slow encoder may delay that heartbeat until the existing stale-session limit applies. This patch does not add live confirmation or guarantee synchronization with the separate FFmpeg camera video. Future confirmation must validate current in-memory source state and use the actual analyzed snapshot.
